<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-accelerometers">
    <title>Accelerometers</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to measure acceleration with the
            LSM303AGR, a three-axis accelerometer, and to use what it measures
            to compute how a board is tilted — the same computation your phone
            does when it decides to rotate its screen.  The sensor is an I2C
            device, so everything we built in <xref ref="ch-i2c"/> carries
            over: we will wire it onto the same two-wire bus the seven-segment
            display is already using, verify the link, look at what is inside
            the sensor, and complete a device driver for it.
            <xref ref="sec-accel-reference"/> collects the register map, the
            data-format arithmetic, and the floating-point details for looking
            up later.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Wire a second device onto the I2C bus and verify the link by reading its WHO_AM_I register.</li>
                <li>Debug an I2C bus with the AD2's logic analyzer and protocol decoder.</li>
                <li>Explain how a MEMS accelerometer converts acceleration into a digital reading.</li>
                <li>Convert the sensor's raw output — 16-bit, left-justified, two's complement — into milli-g.</li>
                <li>Complete, test, and package a device driver for the LSM303AGR.</li>
                <li>Compute tilt angles from gravity's projection, using floating-point arithmetic on a processor without floating-point hardware.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

